## Gate shapes (named)

The old pack named only `process_exit(1)`. Live shapes:

### A. `process_exit(1)`

Caller dies. Used in `*_stubs.oo`, some monofile primitive entries,
and `std/crypto/kyber.oo`. Fail-closed **where it exists**.

### B. `"UNIMPLEMENTED_RESIDUAL"`

String return (22 chars). Product `ooda/std/hash/sha256.oo` does
`return "UNIMPLEMENTED_RESIDUAL"`. Caller gets a string, **not** an
exit. The verify block asserts that exact string.

### C. CHS internal

Product `ooda/std/core/crypto.oo` `sha256` / `hmac_sha256` call
`crypto_*_internal`. `ooda/std/hash/md5.oo` and `sha1.oo` same. AES
returns the C-runtime string `STUB_FAIL_CLOSED` (not an exit).
**F39:** `md5.oo` / `sha1.oo` are non-crypto checksums (RFC 1321 / 3174); collisions exist.

### D. No gate (named so nobody pretends it exits)

Live `*_ops.oo` helpers. Also fail-open / synthetic:

- `dilithium_sign_message` / `dilithium_verify_signature` — djb-style
  hash + string `"sig"`; verify can return `is_valid: true`
- `groth16_verifier_verify_structural` — sets `is_valid: true` and
  `pairing_satisfied: true` on format checks
- `secp256k1_point_create` — `is_valid: true` on 32-byte length
- `plonk_verifier_verify_proof` — `Int` modular theatre; can return true

